## 5. Adversarial Testing Results

| My message | Model said | Should be | Why it failed |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cl@im your fr33 g1ft card now | Actual prediction | Spam | Spelling tricks hide familiar words. |
| Not free, just confirming lunch at 1pm | Actual prediction | Ham | Model may ignore negation. |
| Good afternoon. Kindly verify your account to receive the complimentary reward. | Actual prediction | Spam | Polite wording disguises suspicious intent. |
| Your parcel is waiting. Pay the small release fee at bit.ly/claim-now | Actual prediction | Spam | Unfamiliar URL and payment wording. |
| You have won a £500 voucher! Text WIN to 80085 now to claim. | Actual prediction | Spam | Prize and urgency techniques. |

Replace each prediction with the actual output from the notebook. These are expected labels, not assumed model results.

## 6. Techniques Used to Fool the Model

1. Spelling manipulation: Replacing letters with numbers and symbols.
2. Negation: Using words such as "not" to change the meaning.
3. Businesslike language: Using polite and professional wording.
4. URL manipulation: Including shortened links and payment requests.
5. Prize manipulation: Using rewards, urgency, and promotional language.

## 7. What I Learned

I learned that a machine learning model can achieve good accuracy but still make mistakes when messages use unfamiliar spelling or misleading wording.

I also learned that traditional Naive Bayes models mainly recognize word patterns and do not fully understand context, negation, or the actual intention of a message.

## 8. Project Files

- spam_filter.ipynb: Model implementation, training, accuracy evaluation, and adversarial testing.
- README.md: Project documentation and experimental results.
- requirements.txt: Required Python libraries.

## 9. Conclusion

This experiment demonstrates the strengths and limitations of a basic machine learning spam detector. Adversarial testing helps identify weaknesses and shows why more advanced text-processing techniques may be necessary.